  "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\u222c}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\u222e}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={\u222c}_{D}(\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/c4dd4d03bdfcf6c3d597e2fdef8681e4f3316b3f276cb9967933b61b0dc90976.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x-\\sqrt{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=y-\\sqrt{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}=1,\\,\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\u222e}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}(\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}(1+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}\\,dx \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\,dx \\\\  & =2(-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(0-(-1)) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
